# LF in the working tree on every platform, not just in the committed blobs.
#
# `text=auto` alone is not enough when core.autocrlf is on: it normalises what
# is committed but still smudges LF into CRLF on checkout. That is invisible in
# `git status` and harmless for most files, but not for one that is read by
# something other than git — a shell script destined for a Unix host arrives
# with a CR on the shebang line and fails with "bad interpreter", and the error
# names neither the line endings nor the checkout that caused them.
#
# eol=lf pins the working tree instead. Nothing here needs CRLF: PowerShell, mpv
# and every config format in this repository read LF without complaint.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the file types that actually ship somewhere.
*.ps1   text eol=lf
*.psd1  text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.conf  text eol=lf
*.lua   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# Binary, so git neither diffs them nor touches their bytes.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.zip   binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
